{% import '_common/networkpolicy-macros.j2' as np with context %}
{% import '_common/app-netpol-gate.j2' as gate with context %}
{% if gate.active('console') %}
{# Console. Ingress-only: the namespace egress cannot be listed (GitLab, Harbor, Nexus, SonarQube, Vault, Argo CD
   and Keycloak of this DSC, Kubernetes API of the project clusters, S3, HTTP proxy, plugin downloads from public
   hosts, Barman backups of pg-cluster-console to S3), so no DNS policy and no Egress side.
   Ingress sources (chart dso-console, nameOverride <dsc>-cpn-console, pods
   app.kubernetes.io/name=<dsc>-cpn-console-<part>): ingress controller to the client (/) and the strangler (/api,
   swagger), both 8080 (Services 80 -> 8080), CNPG operator to the pg-cluster-console status port 8000, metrics
   scraper to the pg-cluster-console metrics port 9187 (the chart has no ServiceMonitor). Server 8080 and backend
   3001 are only reached from the strangler, inside the namespace (intra), as is PostgreSQL 5432.
   The CNPG operator peer is np.cnpg_operator_peer (local namespace, else global.networkpolicies.cnpgOperatorNamespace,
   else any namespace with the operator pod labels); the scrape rule follows np.scrape_on; ACME HTTP-01 solver pods
   are opened by np.acme_solver. #}
{% set app = 'console' %}
{% set name = dsc_name ~ '-cpn-console' %}
{{ np.intra(app, egress=false) }}
{{ np.acme_solver(app) }}
{{ np.ingress_controller('console-client', {'app.kubernetes.io/name': name ~ '-client'}, [8080]) }}
{{ np.ingress_controller('console-strangler', {'app.kubernetes.io/name': name ~ '-strangler'}, [8080]) }}
{{ np.from_namespaces(app, 'cnpg-operator', {'cnpg.io/cluster': 'pg-cluster-console'}, [np.cnpg_operator_peer() | from_json], [8000]) }}
{% if np.scrape_on() %}
{{ np.scrape(app, {}, [9187]) }}
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
